Well, you seem to be under the impression that I
_race_ for mines town.  Quite the opposite is true.

I dawdle on level one, not for me, but to buff up my
pet by letting it kill lots of easy monsters, don't
usually descend from there until I have a large dog,
horse, or cat, or even several large pets.  That way
I can afford to explore each level of mines pretty
thoroughly.  I also tend to explore the main dungeon
through and including level four before taking on
mines, even if the mines entrance is on level two.
This gives me more "stuff", and a better chance to
have ID-d the identify scroll prior to taking on
mines.

Once into mines, again slow and steady wins the
race, so I explore every corner of every level,
even if I run into the stairs down early.  This
allows me to try on _lots_ of armor my pet has let
me know will come back off again.  It isn't at all
unusual to have one or even two pieces of +4 armor,
and an elven or dwarvish mithral coat, before I hit
Mines Town.

As another poster mentioned, even if playing a spell
caster, it is a good idea to go into Mines Town
dressed like a tank, especially if you aren't
attempting to kill anything. You can always throw
the tinfoil away later. Since in my case I'm
targeting doing that still at XL one, the issue of
"did I use my spells enough to upgrade them" never
arises; that all happens _after_ protection has been
purchased.

The nice thing about all this is that my improved
AC, and my pet's, or more usually pets', improved
hit dice status, doesn't make the monsters generated
any tougher; if the entrance to mines is on level
two, I'll rarely even see mordor orcs in Mines Town,
and if I do, they keep my pets well fed.

By the time I've bought protection, with this game
plan, I'm more likely to be AC -18 to -20 than
your estimate of AC -10.

Having gained protection, I climb back to DLVL _1_
to start killing monsters on my own, so that except
for the turns counter and my exceptional AC, I'm
no different than the beginning PC, just much better
able to survive.

